

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that collapses the scope a lot. No accounts, no server, one browser.

  It also exposes the crux of the feature. In a single-user local demo, the only actor is the person sitting there.
  Notifying you about edits you just made yourself is noise, so the design lives or dies on [1mwhat else changes a task[0m.
  That determines whether there's a real feature here or just a toast on your own click.

  - [1mTime-based[0m — due dates and reminders fire. The "change" is the clock, not a person. Genuinely useful single-user,
    needs no fake actors, and is the only source that still makes sense when you're the only one there.
  - [1mSimulated activity[0m — a fake background actor mutates tasks so the notification machinery has something to react to.
    Good for demoing the UI to someone; the notifications are theater, and you'd throw the generator away when a real
    backend arrives.
  - [1mCross-tab / external edits[0m — the same data changed in another tab or by an import. Real changes, real
    reconciliation, but it's a sync problem wearing a notifications hat, and it's the most work.
  - [1mYour own edits, with undo[0m — every change toasts with an undo affordance. Not really notifications; it's an activity
    log. Cheap and honestly useful, but it doesn't answer "tasks they care about."

  I'd recommend time-based: it's the only one where a single-user app has something to tell you that you don't already
  know. But "tasks they care about" sounds like you had watching/subscribing in mind, which points at the second or
  third.
